README-GCC -- using the gcc toolchains on this diskVerified 2026-08-24 by compiling, linking and RUNNING a C program and a C++program on this disk.  Both work.  Neither works out of the box, and thisfile is the difference.WHAT IS HERE  /dd/CMDS/GCC2      GNU C 2.5.6.  gcc2 drives C, gpp drives C++.  The                     passes are cccp2, cc2, cc1plus, cc2plus and collect.  /dd/CMDS/GCC139    GNU C 1.39, the same shape, passes already named                     gcc_cc1, gcc_cccp, gcc_cc1plus, gcc_collect.  /dd/GNULIB         os9lib.l, gnulib.l, gpp.l, libgpp.l  /dd/DEFS/GCC2      the gcc header set  /dd/DEFS/os9lib    the C headers matching GNULIB/os9lib.l  /dd/SRC/gcc142     the source of GCC2's gcc driver, which reports                     `gcc version 1.42' -- the OS-9 port of GNU C 1.42WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND CANNOT BE  r68 (assembler), l68 (linker), clibn.l and cstart.r are Microware's and  are not in this collection.  gcc compiles C to ASSEMBLY and then hands  that to r68, and hands the objects to l68.  Without those two you can  preprocess and compile and get no further.  So: you need your own OS-9 SDK.  Nothing on this disk can replace it,  and the six assemblers here (as0, as09, as1, as4, as5, as11) will not  do -- they are 6800, 6809, 6801, 6804, 6805 and 68HC11 cross-assemblers,  not 68000 ones.HOW THE DRIVERS FIND THEIR PASSES -- THE SURPRISE  gcc2 and gpp do not look in the directory they were started from.  Each  has two prefixes compiled into it, and it looks ONLY there:      gcc2   /dd/CMDS/gcc_<pass>    and  /h0/CMDS/gcc_<pass>      gpp    /dd/CMDS/gpp_<pass>    and  /h0/CMDS/gpp_<pass>  Note that they are DIFFERENT prefixes.  Anything else a driver runs --  r68, l68, del -- it forks by bare name, which OS-9 looks up in the  EXECUTION directory, normally /dd/CMDS.  The passes ship in /dd/CMDS/GCC2, which is none of those places, so a  bare attempt stops immediately:      $ /dd/CMDS/GCC2/gcc2 -c hello.c      gcc2: Can't fork 'cccp2': 000:221 module not found  That is not a broken binary.  It is a program looking where it was told  to look and not finding anything.WHAT TO DO  1.  Put your SDK's r68 and l68 where a bare name is found -- /dd/CMDS,      or /h0/CMDS if you would rather keep this disk read-only.  2.  Nothing.  The five copies this used to ask you to make ARE NOW ON      THE DISK, installed 2026-08-27:          /dd/CMDS/gcc_cccp2      gcc2's preprocessor          /dd/CMDS/gcc_cc2        gcc2's C compiler proper          /dd/CMDS/gpp_cccp       the same preprocessor under gpp's name          /dd/CMDS/gpp_cc1plus    gpp's C++ compiler proper          /dd/CMDS/gpp_collect    gpp's global-constructor collector      cccp2 is present twice on purpose: gcc2 forks it as cccp2 and gpp      forks it as cccp.  The originals are still in /dd/CMDS/GCC2; these      are byte copies, so the two directories cannot disagree.      GCC139 is deliberately NOT installed this way.  Its passes are      already named gcc_cc1, gcc_cccp, gcc_cc1plus and gcc_collect, and      gcc_cc1plus would collide with GCC2's.  To use GCC139 instead, copy      /dd/CMDS/GCC139's passes into /dd/CMDS yourself and remove GCC2's.  3.  Put clibn.l and cstart.r where l68 will find them -- /dd/LIB or      /h0/LIB.  Then both of these work:      /dd/CMDS/GCC2/gcc2 -o hello hello.c      /dd/CMDS/GCC2/gpp  -o hello hello.cc  GCC139 cannot be installed at the same time as GCC2 this way: its  gcc_cc1plus and GCC2's would want the same name.  Pick one.HEADERS  <stdio.h> in /dd/DEFS/GCC2 forwards to /dd/DEFS/os9lib/stdio.h, which is  the header that matches GNULIB/os9lib.l, the C library this disk ships  for gcc.  It used to forward to /dd/DEFS/stdio.h, which is Microware's  and is not on this disk, so nothing that included <stdio.h> could be  compiled at all.  Fixed 2026-08-24.  os9lib's header has no SEEK_SET, SEEK_CUR or SEEK_END; DEFS/GCC2/stdio.h  adds them.ONE COSMETIC FAILURE YOU WILL SEE  gpp removes its temporary files by forking `del', and this disk has rm  and no del.  You will see      gpp: Program del got fatal signal 221.  AFTER your program has already been written.  The output is fine; the  temporaries are not cleaned up.  Supply a del from your SDK if it  bothers you.